WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.067
Streaming responses.

2
00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:05.545
Nobody wants to stare at a spinner for twenty seconds.

3
00:00:05.545 --> 00:00:08.900
Streaming fixes that without making the model any faster.

4
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.100
Server-Sent Events push tokens to the client as the model writes them.

5
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.133
It's plain HTTP, using the event stream content type.

6
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:22.100
Each message is just a data line followed by a blank line.

7
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.433
Look at the difference.

8
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:28.492
Without streaming, the user waits for the entire twelve seconds.

9
00:00:28.492 --> 00:00:32.133
With streaming, the first words appear in about half a second.

10
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:34.333
Here's the chain.

11
00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:38.367
The provider's API already streams to you using SSE.

12
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:42.300
Your API passes those tokens along in the same format.

13
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.633
And the browser reads them with its built in event source, which even reconnects automatically.

14
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:50.200
Three things to remember.

15
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.313
It's one way, server to client.

16
00:00:52.313 --> 00:00:56.700
For two way traffic you'd reach for WebSockets, but chat rarely needs that.

17
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.767
Time to first token becomes your headline latency metric, instead of total time.

18
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:03.436
And it's browser native.

19
00:01:03.436 --> 00:01:05.433
Debug it from a terminal with curl.

20
00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:08.367
Here it is in FastAPI.

21
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:14.267
Inside a generator, we loop over tokens from the model and yield each one as a data line.

22
00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.833
When it finishes, we yield a done marker.

23
00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:21.933
Then we wrap the generator in a streaming response with the event stream content type.

24
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:24.367
Here's a real case.

25
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:29.467
Answers about old mission failure reports take about fifteen seconds end to end.

26
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:35.567
After switching the chat endpoint to SSE, the first tokens land while the model is still writing.

27
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:39.950
Traces now track time to first token, zero point six seconds.

28
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:44.033
Same compute, same cost, and the slow complaints disappeared.

29
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:46.286
Let's recap.

30
00:01:46.286 --> 00:01:49.267
SSE pushes tokens over plain HTTP.

31
00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.500
It's one way, server to client.

32
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.100
Time to first token becomes the key latency metric.

33
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.280
Same compute, far better perceived speed.

34
00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.333
Next up: Flask.
